Information Disclosure via Environment Enumeration
It was observed that the application exposes multiple environment details such as production, QA, and test configurations in accessible files.
This allows users to identify internal server names, database mappings, and infrastructure details.
Verbose Error Message Leading to Information Disclosure
It was observed that the application returns detailed error messages including stack traces when invalid input is provided.
These messages reveal internal application logic, file paths, and backend technology details.
Insecure Storage of Encrypted Credentials with Co-located Key Material
It was observed that encrypted credential files are stored along with their corresponding key and IV files in the same directory.
This exposes all components required for decryption and weakens the effectiveness of encryption.
Unsigned DLL (Code Integrity Issue)
It was observed that application DLL files are not digitally signed and are loaded during execution without validation.
This allows potential tampering or replacement of DLLs without detection.
Hardcoded Database Credentials
It was observed that database credentials are stored in plaintext within configuration files accessible via shared paths.
This exposes sensitive authentication information without any encryption or protection.
Hardcoded LDAP Credentials
It was observed that LDAP credentials are hardcoded in configuration files used by the application.
These credentials are accessible in plaintext, leading to potential unauthorized directory access.
Insecure LDAP Communication (Non-LDAPS)
It was observed that the application uses LDAP communication over non-secure channels without encryption.
This exposes sensitive credentials and data to interception over the network.
